artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a suspension consisting of rigid solid particles dispersed in a viscous fluid. In a homogenization limit (where the particle size and inter-particle distance are much smaller than the macroscopic length scale), one seeks an effective continuum description in which the local microstructure (e.g. particle volume fraction, orientation distribution, and pair-correlation data) evolves with the macroscopic flow. Develop a homogenized continuum theory for such suspensions that:

(a) admits and rigorously describes the formation of "particle shocks", i.e. discontinuities or concentration singularities in the microstructural fields (such as the volume fraction or orientation distribution) that arise from the transport of the microstructure by the effective flow, including the limiting case of particle-particle contacts/collisions;

(b) simultaneously incorporates compressibility effects, either of the suspending fluid or arising from changes in packing of the granular phase, in the macroscopic balance laws (mass, momentum, energy) and in the effective constitutive relations;

(c) yields well-posed evolution equations for the coupled fields (density, velocity, stress, and microstructural descriptors), and identifies conditions under which classical or weak solutions exist beyond the onset of such microstructural singularities.

Provide such a theory, or prove obstructions to its existence.

